  "conclusion": "Manufacturer 207 exhibits the most dangerous hidden dependency: it has the highest receipt_reach (0.0387) among top manufacturers (E3) yet represents only 0.32% of total revenue (E3), making its supply impact disproportionate to its size. One customer relies on it for 50%+ of their store spend (E5), and it sells across 37 product classes with no sole-source classes but high class diversification risk (E4). Its stores are concentrated in a single state (TN, 6 stores, $16.47M revenue) (E7) while customers span at least 15 states plus a null-state group (270 customers, $465.7K revenue) (E7), creating geographic exposure. Compared to manufacturer 158, which has receipt_reach 0.035 and revenue_share 0.0031 (E3) with 4 customers at >=50% reliance (E6), manufacturer 207\u2019s combination of top receipt_reach, minimal revenue share, and broad class presence makes it the highest hidden risk.",
  "why": [
   "Manufacturer 207 ranks #1 in receipt_reach at 0.0387 (E3) and revenue_share 0.0032 (E3) across 240,000 total receipts, yet its revenue share is only 0.32% of total store sales ($16.9M of $5.14B), confirming a hidden dependency (E3).",
   "One customer relies on manufacturer 207 for half or more of their store spend (customers_50pct_plus = 1 out of 90,858 total customers) (E5).",
   "Manufacturer 207 sells in 37 named product classes plus 1 blank category/class gap (E4), spreading risk across many categories without being the sole maker of any single class, which amplifies disruption potential if any class faces supply interruption (E4).",
   "Geographic exposure: stores limited to Tennessee (6 stores, $16.47M revenue) (E7), but customer states include TX, GA, KY, VA, NC, KS, IA, IL, TN, NE, IN, MO, MI, MN, plus a null-state group (270 customers, $465.7K revenue) (E7), creating store-concentration vs. customer-dispersion risk (E7).",
   "Manufacturer 158 has receipt_reach 0.035 and revenue_share 0.0031 (E3) with 4 customers at >=50% reliance on its products (E6), but manufacturer 207\u2019s receipt_reach is higher relative to its revenue footprint, making its dependency more hidden given the disproportionate receipt reach compared to revenue share (E3, E6)."
